Thompson Seedless grapes, also known as Sultana, are a seedless grape variety that is botanically classified as *Vitis vinifera*. They are characterized by their pale green color, oval shape, and lack of seeds. These grapes are native to the Levant region, which later became part of the Ottoman Empire, and they have been extensively cultivated in regions such as Turkey, particularly around İzmir, where they are known as İzmir üzümü. Thompson Seedless grapes are widely grown and used for both fresh consumption and raisin production. Their origin can be traced back to ancient times, and they are considered one of the most popular seedless grape varieties in the United States.
