Outline the uses of Thompson seedless grapes in food, wine, and other industries, along with their nutritional profile and cultural significance.
Thompson Seedless grapes are widely used in various industries. In food, they are primarily used to produce raisins, with nearly half of all Sun-Maid raisins being made from this variety. They are also used as an ingredient in bakeries, cereal companies, and as raisin paste and concentrate. Additionally, Thompson Seedless grapes are sometimes diverted from the raisin or table market to produce white grape juice, particularly in California. In the wine industry, they are used to make non-alcoholic wine, as demonstrated by Thomas Bramwell Welch's contribution to pasteurizing grape juice to halt fermentation. Culturally, Thompson Seedless grapes hold significance due to their popularity in supermarkets and their role in various culinary traditions. Nutritionally, Thompson Seedless grapes are rich in antioxidants, fiber, and vitamins, contributing to their health benefits.
