Identify the primary language used for Catholic Mass before the 1960s and explain the historical and liturgical context that influenced this linguistic choice.
The primary language used for Catholic Mass before the 1960s was Latin. This linguistic choice was rooted in the historical and liturgical context of the Roman Catholic Church, which viewed Latin as a sacred and universal language. Latin served as a lingua franca across Europe, facilitating communication among diverse regions and cultures. It was considered a symbol of unity and continuity with the early Church, as well as a means to preserve theological and doctrinal purity. The use of Latin in liturgy was also tied to its role as a dead language, which was believed to elevate the spiritual and intellectual aspects of worship. However, this practice began to change with the reforms introduced by the Second Vatican Council (1962–1965), which encouraged the use of vernacular languages alongside Latin to make the Mass more accessible to the faithful.
