Explain the function and structural characteristics of the Pyramids of Malpighi in relation to their anatomical location.
The Pyramids of Malpighi, also known as renal pyramids, are cone-shaped structures located in the renal medulla of the kidney. Structurally, they are composed of straight parallel segments of nephrons and collecting ducts, which give them a striped appearance. Each pyramid has a broad base that faces the renal cortex and an apex (papilla) that points toward the renal pelvis. The base of each pyramid originates at the corticomedullary border, while the apex terminates in a papilla within a minor calyx.

Functionally, the renal pyramids play a crucial role in the urinary system by facilitating the concentration of urine. They are involved in the final stages of urine formation, where the collecting ducts transport urine from the nephrons to the renal pelvis. The structural arrangement of the pyramids allows for efficient drainage of urine from the kidney to the urinary tract.
