The phrase "Charley horse" was first used in the context of sports, specifically baseball. The term originated from an incident involving Chicago outfielder George Gore, who experienced a sudden leg cramp or strain while running to second base, similar to how a horse named Charley had previously suffered. This incident prompted Joe Quest to comment, "There's your old Charley horse," which led players to adopt the term to describe such sudden leg cramps or strains. The earliest known account of the phrase's origin in reference to a sports injury was published in the "Boston Globe" in 1886, where it was also attributed to Quest. This context highlights the role of a specific event in a sport (baseball) and a figure (Joe Quest) in the development of the phrase.
