The religious dietary laws that govern what foods are permissible for Jewish people are known as "kashrut." These laws dictate which foods are considered kosher (permissible) and which are not. Key aspects of kashrut include restrictions on certain animals, such as requiring mammals to have split hooves and chew their cud, and prohibitions on mixing meat and dairy. Foods that adhere to these laws are termed kosher, while those that do not are referred to as treifah or treif.
