Identify the defining characteristics of Rafflesia Arnoldii and determine what attribute makes it the world's largest in that category
The defining characteristics of Rafflesia arnoldii include being a parasitic flowering plant known for producing the largest individual flower on Earth. It is commonly called the corpse lily or corpse flower due to its strong and unpleasant odor resembling decaying flesh. Rafflesia arnoldii is endemic to the rainforests of Sumatra and possibly Borneo. The attribute that makes it the world's largest in its category is the size of its individual flower, which surpasses other plants with larger flowering organs, such as the titan arum (Amorphophallus titanum) and talipot palm (Corypha umbraculifera), which are technically clusters of many flowers.
