Determine the significance of Rafflesia Arnoldii as a national emblem and its cultural or symbolic importance to Indonesia
Rafflesia arnoldii holds significant cultural and symbolic importance in Indonesia. It is recognized as one of the national flowers, alongside the white jasmine (Jasminum sambac) and the moon orchid (Phalaenopsis amabilis). This recognition was formalized on World Environment Day on 5 June 1990 by President Soeharto. Rafflesia arnoldii is also designated as a "rare flower" through Presidential Decree No. 4 in 1993, highlighting its rarity and ecological value.

Symbolically, Rafflesia arnoldii represents the uniqueness and rich biodiversity of Indonesia. Its inclusion as a national emblem underscores the country's commitment to preserving its natural heritage and promoting environmental awareness. Additionally, it reflects the cultural reverence for nature, as seen in traditional practices where flowers are used in floral offerings for spirits and deities, often present during funerals, which imbue them with mystical and sacred properties.
