Identify the year when the pound coin was introduced as a replacement for the Bank of England £1 banknote, including the historical context and reasons for the change.
The pound coin was introduced in 1983 as a replacement for the Bank of England £1 banknote. The decision to replace the note with a coin was driven by several factors. Firstly, notes had a much shorter lifespan compared to coins, with notes lasting on average nine months in circulation while coins could last up to forty years. Secondly, the growing vending industry benefited from the use of coins, as they were more suitable for automated machines. Additionally, the introduction of the coin aimed to improve the efficiency and practicality of everyday transactions. The new cupro-nickel one-pound coin was introduced on 21 April 1983, and the Bank of England £1 note ceased to be legal tender on 11 March 1988.
