In organisms that function as both sexes (hermaphrodites), they possess both male and female reproductive organs, enabling them to produce gametes typically associated with both sexes. This dual functionality allows for internal or external fertilization, depending on the species. In some cases, hermaphrodites can self-fertilize, although this is less common and often avoided to maintain genetic diversity. The reproductive mechanism involves the production of eggs and sperm, which may be released into the environment or transferred between individuals. The life cycle of such organisms typically includes a larval stage, which may vary in complexity, followed by metamorphosis into the adult form. These organisms often have specialized structures for reproduction and may exhibit unique strategies to ensure successful fertilization and offspring survival.
