Identify the administrative structure of South Yorkshire and determine which town serves as its central governing authority.
South Yorkshire is a metropolitan county in England, consisting of four metropolitan boroughs: Barnsley, Doncaster, Rotherham, and Sheffield. The administrative structure of South Yorkshire involves these four boroughs, which function as unitary authorities. The functions of the former South Yorkshire County Council were devolved to these boroughs, with many services being jointly administered by joint authorities that include representatives from all four councils. There is no single central governing authority for South Yorkshire; instead, governance is shared among the four metropolitan boroughs.
