The Domesday Book was commissioned by King William the Conqueror in 1086. Its purpose was to conduct a comprehensive survey of much of England and parts of Wales, primarily to assess the properties of farmsteads and landholders in order to tax them sufficiently. This survey provided a detailed record of land ownership, resources, and administrative counties, serving as a foundational document for taxation and governance.
