Identify the historical and religious context of the crucifixion of Jesus and St Peter, including their roles, times, and locations
The crucifixion of Jesus occurred in 1st-century Judea, most likely between AD 30 and 33. It is described in the four canonical gospels and attested to by other ancient sources, established as a historical event confirmed by non-Christian sources. According to the canonical gospels, Jesus was arrested, tried by the Sanhedrin, sentenced by Pontius Pilate to be scourged, and finally crucified by the Romans. The crucifixion took place on Golgotha, a hill outside Jerusalem. 

St Peter, one of Jesus' disciples, played a significant role in the early Christian movement. He is remembered as the first Pope and was instrumental in spreading the new theology concerning Jesus throughout the Roman Empire. St Peter's connection to the crucifixion of Jesus is through his role as a follower and leader in the early church, though he was not directly involved in the crucifixion itself. The historical and religious context of these events is deeply rooted in the spread of Christianity following Jesus' death and resurrection, which shaped Western civilization and culture.
