Explain the definition and working mechanism of an ISP in the context of computing, including its role in providing internet connectivity.
An Internet Service Provider (ISP) is an organization that provides services for accessing, using, or participating in the Internet. Its primary role is to establish connectivity between end-users and the global Internet. ISPs offer various services such as Internet access, Internet transit, domain name registration, web hosting, Usenet service, and colocation.

The working mechanism of an ISP involves connecting individual networks at different levels of scope, enabling worldwide connectivity. At the top of the routing hierarchy are tier 1 networks, which are large telecommunication companies that exchange traffic directly with each other via high-speed fiber optic cables under peering agreements. Tier 2 and lower-level networks purchase Internet transit from other providers to connect to the broader Internet.

ISPs facilitate communication by providing the necessary infrastructure and services for users to access and interact with the Internet. They serve as intermediaries between end-users and the larger Internet ecosystem, ensuring seamless connectivity and reliable service delivery.
