The dynastic family to which Cleopatra belonged is the Ptolemaic dynasty. This dynasty was established by Ptolemy I Soter, a general of Alexander the Great, and ruled Egypt from 305 BC until the Roman conquest in 30 BC. The Ptolemaic rulers were of Greek origin, having descended from the Macedonian royal family, and they maintained a complex relationship with the native Egyptian population. They adopted many aspects of Egyptian culture and religion, including the worship of the goddess Isis, which helped them gain acceptance among the local populace. Cleopatra VII, the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt, is renowned for her political alliances with Roman leaders such as Julius Caesar and Mark Antony, which played a significant role in the eventual downfall of the Ptolemaic dynasty and the integration of Egypt into the Roman Empire.
