Explain the defining features of this art style, such as techniques, subject matter, and influence on later artistic developments.
The defining features of Impressionism include relatively small, thin, yet visible brush strokes, open composition, and an emphasis on the accurate depiction of light in its changing qualities. The art movement also focuses on ordinary subject matter and incorporates "movement" as a crucial element of human perception and experience. Additionally, Impressionism is characterized by unusual visual angles. This art style originated with a group of Paris-based artists during the 1870s and 1880s and faced opposition from the conventional art community in France. The name of the style derives from the title of a painting by Claude Monet. Impressionism influenced many later artistic developments, including the work of artists like Kazimir Malevich, who was inspired by Impressionism among other movements.
