Explain the general behavior of wind patterns in low-pressure systems and how they differ between hemispheres
In low-pressure systems, wind patterns are characterized by inward flow toward the center of the system. In the Northern Hemisphere, this inward flow is deflected to the right due to the Coriolis effect, resulting in counterclockwise circulation. In contrast, in the Southern Hemisphere, the inward flow is deflected to the left, leading to clockwise circulation. These differences arise from the Earth's rotation and the Coriolis force, which influences wind direction based on the hemisphere.
